# CHANGELOG.md is fully machine-generated (git-cliff, see cliff.toml) and
# gets regenerated + committed independently on both `main` and `develop`
# (.github/workflows/changelog.yml) -- so a develop<->main merge can hit a
# genuine textual conflict where both sides changed the same lines
# differently, even though neither side's exact content matters: the very
# next push to whichever branch wins re-regenerates it from scratch anyway.
# `union` is git's own built-in low-level merge strategy (no custom
# `.git/config` driver registration needed, unlike `merge=ours` -- that
# distinction matters here since a driver registered only in a local
# `.git/config` can't be honored by GitHub's own server-side PR merge,
# which doesn't read it). Not confirmed whether GitHub's merge backend
# actually applies attribute-based strategies like this one; it's free
# insurance for local `git merge`/`git pull` either way, and worst case
# a develop<->main PR still needs the same one-line fix this file's
# history already needed once: regenerate CHANGELOG.md fresh
# (`git-cliff --config cliff.toml --output CHANGELOG.md`) instead of
# hand-resolving the conflict markers.
CHANGELOG.md merge=union
